The UDP protocol reveals that: when attempting to reach the
www.yummyrecipesforme.com
The tcpdump reveals that the error is related to issues contacting the DNS server
“203.0.113.2.domain”
Every first two lines of every log event show my browser trying to contact the DNS server.
On the third and fourth lines of the log we get an ICMP response: UDP Port 53
unreachable.
The port 53 is associated with DNS traffic - protocol to translate an internet domain name
into an IP address.
Based on this information and also the “+” sign after the query identification number
“35084” that indicated flags with the UDP message and in addition the “A?” symbol
indicates flags performing DNS operations.
The most likely issue is: No response from DNS server for “www.yummyrecipesforme.com”


Time incident occurred: 1:24 p.m.
The IT team became aware of the incident when client company reported they can not
reach the website and get an error “Destination port unreachable” after waiting for the page
to load.
The actions taken by the IT department to investigate the incident:
First I attempted to visit the yummy recipes website, then I got the error.
I loaded my network analyzer tool tcpdump, and reloaded the website.
I realized my computer was sending a query to the DNS server, assumption supported by
flags.
Then I received ICMP packets that say “udp port 53 unreachable length …”
I was trying to load the website a couple more times and got the same error. The next
steps would be to verify if there's a problem with the DNS server being shutdown, or the
firewall blocking unauthorized traffic to port 53 as part of network security filters.
